"800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/><a:tailEnd len="med" w="med" type="none"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="An image depicting the entry of glycerol into the glycolytic pathway. The chain begins with glycerol, where CH2OH bonds down to C, which bonds left to HO, right to H, and down to CH2OH. An arrow points down, represented as glycerol kinase (in blue) to L-glycerol 3-phosphate. To the right of the arrow is a curved arrow pointing from ATP to ADP. L-glycerol 3-phosphate begins with CH2OH which bonds down to C. C bonds left to HO, right to H, and down to CH2. CH2 bonds right to, which bonds right to P. P bonds down to O-, right to O-, and double bonds up to O. Reversible arrows are below that, represented as glycerol 3-phosphate dehydrogenase, point to dihydroxyacetone phosphate. Another arrow is to the right, pointing from NAD+ to NADH [plus] H+. Dihydroxyacetone phosphate begins with CH2OH bonds down to C, which double bonds left to O and down to CH2. CH2 bonds right to O, which bonds right to P. P bonds down to O-, right to O-, and double bonds up to O. Reversible arrows, represented as triose phophate isomerase (in blue), are below this and point to D-glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate. This starts with C bonding up and left to H, double bonding up and right to O, and down to C. C then bonds left to H, right to OH, and down to CH2, which bonds right to O, which bonds to P. P bonds down to O-, right to O-, and double bonds up to O. An arrow below this points to glycolysis. " id="188" name="Google Shape;188;p26"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="5105400" y="887412"/><a:ext cx="3657600" cy="5808662"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><